6 Delete one member who has never borrowed a book (check the LOANS table yourself
first), then rename the LOANS table to BOOK_LOANS , and finally run COMMIT .

Once the data is ready, the library team sends you the following requests. Write
a separate query for each one:
7 “We need to see all the member data.”
8 “The mobile screen only needs the book title, author, and price.” Name the columns
"Book Title" , "Author" , and "Price" .
9 “Give us the list of cities where we have members, each city once.”
10 “Show every book with its price including 14% VAT, rounded to two decimals.”
11 “Members who live in Cairo.” Then rewrite it with 'cairo' and explain in a comment why
the result changed.
12 “Campaign books: priced above 300 and actually available in the library.”
13 “Books priced from 100 to 500.” Watch the boundary: did the book priced at exactly 500
show up?
14 “Members from Cairo, Giza, or Alexandria.” Write it in two different ways, and say in a
comment which one is cleaner.
15 “Books whose title starts with A , and books whose title ends with y .”
16 “Members with no phone number, since we cannot call them.” In a comment, explain why
phone = NULL does not work.
17 “A catalogue with the most expensive books first; if two prices tie, sort by title
alphabetically.”
18 “A contact sheet for members in Cairo and Giza who have a phone number, most points
first, with column names an employee can understand.” Write it as a single query.
19 “Build a greeting line for every member in this format: Ahmed Hassan — Cairo .” Then show
the name in upper case together with its length, and then the part of the email before the
@ .
20 “Which members joined more than 6 months ago and still have fewer than 200 points?”

2 / 3

21 Predict without running. In a comment, write how many rows each of these queries
will return, then run them and compare:
SELECT * FROM members
WHERE city = 'Cairo' OR city = 'Giza' AND points >= 100;
SELECT * FROM members
WHERE (city = 'Cairo' OR city = 'Giza') AND points >= 100;
SELECT * FROM books WHERE price BETWEEN 500 AND 100;
SELECT title, price * 1.14 AS price_vat
FROM books WHERE price_vat > 500;
If any of them fails with an error, write the error code and its cause.

